6.127.m:
The apsaras said:
Listen, O rākṣasa! I am an apsaras able to change form at will;
I have come from Prayāga, and my name is Kāñcanamālinī.

I did not worship God, the giver of the fruit of the four aims of life;
I did not honor the goddess Durgā, the destroyer of distress;
and Viṣṇu, who removes all sins, was not remembered by me, greedy for pleasures.

Relate all this in full, O great sage; / we shall hear with curiosity this great merit by your grace.

Vasiṣṭha said: / At the holy Plakṣaprasravaṇa on the auspicious bank of the Sarasvatī, / leaning against a mountain, there was his beautiful hermitage

with śāla, tāla, tamāla, bilva, bakula, and pāṭala trees; / with tamarinds, ciribilvas, mangoes, campakas, and kāñcanas;

with karañjas, kovidāras, kesaras, and kuñjarāśanas; / with tilakas, karṇikāras, kumbhas, khadiras, and tindukas;

with vānīras, sālvas, jambīras, pīlus, udumbaras, and vetasas; / with śākoṭas, aṭarūṣas, karahāṭas, and banyan trees;

with ghoṇṭās, kuṭajas, palāśas, and sorrow-removing aśokas; / with jambū, nimba, kadamba, kṣīrikā, and karamardaka trees;

with bījapūras and nāriṅgas, adorned with rows of plantains; / with tasty jackfruits and coconut trees bearing perpetual fruit;

with saptacchadas, tripatras, auspicious śirīṣas and āmalakas; / with karkandhūs, lakucas, akṣas, pāribhadras, and vacās;

With screw-pine, sinduvāra, tagara, kunda, and jasmine, / with lotuses, blue water-lilies, white water-lilies, mālatī, yūthikā, and other flowers,
